  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening transformation towards responsible recruitment in the fishing industry through advocacy, capacity development and engagement withIndonesian stakeholders</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indonesia is a major source country for migrant fishers employed on distant-water fishing vessels, particularly in the tuna industry, which plays a critical role in global seafood supply chains. Despite existing regulatory frameworks, gaps in recruitment practices, licensing systems, and grievance mechanisms continue to expose workers to risks of exploitation and trafficking. Building on previous collaboration between IOM Indonesia and the Seafood Task Force, including stakeholder engagement and multi-stakeholder dialogues, this project aims to strengthen the capacity of key Indonesian stakeholders, including government agencies, private recruitment agency associations (P3MI), civil society organizations, academia, and migrant fisher unions, to promote and apply ethical recruitment principles in line with the ASEAN Declaration and Guidelines on the Placement and Protection of Migrant Fishers. The project will assess and enhance the capacity of recruitment agencies to implement fair and ethical recruitment practices, while increasing awareness among migrant fisher communities and prospective migrant fishers of safe and regular migration pathways, workers&apos; rights and responsibilities, working and living conditions, and available grievance and complaint mechanisms.</narrative>
  </description>
